<div>
    <div class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-orange-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Editor Integration</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            Soli ships a language server, <code class="text-amber-400">soli lsp</code>, spoken over stdio. The VS Code / Cursor and Nova extensions start it, Neovim and Helix take a few lines of configuration, and any editor with a generic LSP client can use it. The server reports its version in <code class="text-amber-400">initialize</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">serverInfo</code>).
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">What the language server does</h2>
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left text-gray-300">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Feature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">What you get</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Diagnostics</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">The lint rules as warnings while you type, and a syntax error as an error at its position. A file under <code class="text-amber-400">app/</code> gets the path-aware rules too (<code class="text-amber-400">security/unfiltered-mass-assignment</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">style/redundant-model-import</code>&hellip;), with the names its auto-loaded neighbours declare in scope, as <code class="text-amber-400">soli lint</code> does. A <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> template has its <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% %&gt;</code> code linted, not its HTML: an unclosed tag or an <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% if %&gt;</code> without its <code class="text-amber-400">end</code> is an error on its line, and a finding covers its whole line.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Hover</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">On a name declared in the file &mdash; at its declaration or at any use &mdash; its kind or declared type (<code class="text-amber-400">greet : function</code>). On a name declared elsewhere in the application &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">Post</code> in a controller, a helper in a view, <code class="text-amber-400">post.summary</code> &mdash; its signature and the file and line it is in. Either way, the <code class="text-amber-400">#</code> comment lines right above a <code class="text-amber-400">def</code>, a class or a constant come with it &mdash; a blank line ends the block. On a common builtin (<code class="text-amber-400">print</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">len</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">json_parse</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">DateTime</code>&hellip;), its signature.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Completion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Keywords, types, the names declared in the file, and the application&rsquo;s classes and functions. After <code class="text-amber-400">.</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">@</code>, the receiver&rsquo;s members &mdash; see below.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Go to definition</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">The declaration of the name under the cursor: in the file first, then anywhere in the application&rsquo;s auto-loaded files (<code class="text-amber-400">app/models</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/controllers</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/services</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/policies</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/mailers</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/middleware</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/jobs</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">app/subscribers</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">app/helpers</code> from a helper or a view). <code class="text-amber-400">Post</code> in a controller lands on <code class="text-amber-400">class Post</code> in <code class="text-amber-400">app/models/post.sl</code>; <code class="text-amber-400">post.summary</code> on the <code class="text-amber-400">def summary</code> of the class <code class="text-amber-400">post</code> holds. A name declared twice answers with both.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">References</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">A local or a parameter: every use in the file. A name the application declares &mdash; a class, a top-level <code class="text-amber-400">def</code>, a helper, a constant, a method or a field &mdash; every use across the application: the <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> files under <code class="text-amber-400">app/</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">config/</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">tests/</code>, and the <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% %&gt;</code> code of the <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> views. Open buffers are read as the editor has them, unsaved changes included. <code class="text-amber-400">#{&hellip;}</code> interpolations count; words in plain strings, comments and template markup do not.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Rename</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">The same occurrences, in one edit across every file they are in. A new name that is not an identifier is refused, and so is renaming a member two classes declare (<code class="text-amber-400">summary</code> in <code class="text-amber-400">Post</code> and in <code class="text-amber-400">Page</code>): <code class="text-amber-400">x.summary</code> cannot be told apart, so the edit would rewrite the other class&rsquo;s calls too.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Document symbols</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">An outline of the file&rsquo;s classes, functions and variables.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Folding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Classes and functions, and multi-line <code class="text-amber-400">{ }</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">( )</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">[ ]</code> (hashes, arrays, long calls).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Formatting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">The whole document, with the same formatter as <code class="text-amber-400">soli fmt</code>. A file that does not parse is left alone.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Code actions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">On a lint finding: rename to the expected case for the naming rules (every occurrence), or insert <code class="text-amber-400"># soli-lint-disable-next-line &lt;rule&gt;</code> above the line.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Formatting, document symbols, folding and code actions are for <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> files; on a <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> template they answer nothing. In a template, completion, references and rename work inside <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% %&gt;</code> tags only, and nowhere does a <code class="text-amber-400">.</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">@</code> in a comment or a string open the member list. A file that is neither <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> nor <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> &mdash; an HTML buffer an editor sends along &mdash; gets nothing from the server.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Member completion</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Typing <code class="text-amber-400">.</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">@</code> asks for the members of what is before it. The server reads that receiver from the text, without parsing &mdash; while you type <code class="text-amber-400">post.</code> the file usually does not parse &mdash; and offers members only when it can tell exactly what the receiver is:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left text-gray-300">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Receiver</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Members offered</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">A class: <code class="text-amber-400">Post.</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Its <code class="text-amber-400">static def</code>s and its <code class="text-amber-400">scope(&quot;&hellip;&quot;)</code>s, inherited ones included, <code class="text-amber-400">new</code>, and &mdash; for a model &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">Model</code>&rsquo;s class methods (<code class="text-amber-400">where</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">find_by</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">create</code>&hellip;).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">An instance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">post.</code> after <code class="text-amber-400">post = Post.find(id)</code> (or <code class="text-amber-400">Post.new(&hellip;)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">find_by</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">first_by</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">first</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">last</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">create</code>), or a parameter typed <code class="text-amber-400">post: Post</code>: the class&rsquo;s methods, fields and <code class="text-amber-400">has_many</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">has_one</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">belongs_to</code> relations, inherited ones included, and &mdash; for a model &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">Model</code>&rsquo;s instance methods (<code class="text-amber-400">save</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">update</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">errors</code>&hellip;).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top"><code class="text-amber-400">@</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">this.</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">The same, for the enclosing class.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">A literal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">&quot;x&quot;.</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">[1, 2].</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">{{&hellip;}}.</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">42.</code>, or a local assigned one: the built-in methods of a string, an array, a hash, a number.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Anything else</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">a.b.</code>, a local of unknown origin: every method name declared in the application, marked <em>receiver not resolved</em>.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The application is indexed once, on the first request that needs it, then kept current file by file: an open buffer on every change (a buffer that does not parse keeps its last good version), a file on save, and &mdash; when the editor supports watching files &mdash; a file changed, added or deleted outside the editor.
    </p>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 mb-12">
        <p class="text-amber-200/90 text-sm">
            <strong class="text-amber-300">Limits:</strong> occurrences are matched by spelling, not by type: within a file, two variables called <code class="text-amber-300">count</code> in two functions count as one name, and across the application <code class="text-amber-300">post.title</code> counts for a <code class="text-amber-300">title</code> field even when <code class="text-amber-300">post</code> is a hash. A name in a string &mdash; <code class="text-amber-300">permit(params, {"title": true})</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">has_many("comments")</code> &mdash; is not renamed. Member completion follows one step: <code class="text-amber-300">post.</code> is resolved, <code class="text-amber-300">post.author.</code> is not. Instance variables a controller sets are not offered in its views.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">VS Code &amp; Cursor</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The extension in <code class="text-amber-400">editors/vscode/</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">solilang.soli-language</code>) starts <code class="text-amber-400">soli lsp</code>, so VS Code, Cursor, VSCodium and Windsurf get everything in the table above, and highlights <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> files and <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> templates. It needs VS Code 1.82 or a build based on it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        It is not on the Marketplace or Open VSX yet. Download <code class="text-amber-400">soli-language.vsix</code> from the artifacts of the <strong class="text-white">vscode extension</strong> workflow run on GitHub, or build it:
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="bash"><code class="language-bash text-sm">cd editors/vscode
npm ci
npm run package        # writes soli-language.vsix
code --install-extension soli-language.vsix
# Cursor:
cursor --install-extension soli-language.vsix</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A 0.2 install upgrades in place: the extension id is unchanged.</p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Languages</h3>
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left text-gray-300">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Language</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Files</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">What the server does</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Soli <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top"><code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Solifile</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Everything above.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-white font-medium align-top">Soli Template <code class="text-amber-400">soli-template</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top"><code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Diagnostics, hover, go-to-definition, and completion inside <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% %&gt;</code>. Highlighting: HTML with Soli inside <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% %&gt;</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;%= %&gt;</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;%- -%&gt;</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;%# %&gt;</code> comments.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Finding <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code></h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">The extension runs the first <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> it finds:</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-4">
        <li>the <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.path</code> setting (an absolute path, <code class="text-amber-400">~/&hellip;</code>, or a name looked up on <code class="text-amber-400">PATH</code>);</li>
        <li><code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> on <code class="text-amber-400">PATH</code>;</li>
        <li><code class="text-amber-400">~/.local/bin/soli</code> (where <code class="text-amber-400">install.sh</code> puts it), <code class="text-amber-400">~/.cargo/bin/soli</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">$CARGO_HOME/bin</code>), <code class="text-amber-400">/usr/local/bin/soli</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">/opt/homebrew/bin/soli</code> on macOS. On Windows: <code class="text-amber-400">soli.exe</code> on <code class="text-amber-400">PATH</code>, then <code class="text-amber-400">%USERPROFILE%\.cargo\bin</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        An editor started from the dock or the start menu often lacks your shell&rsquo;s <code class="text-amber-400">PATH</code>, which is why the install directories are tried. When nothing is found, a notification lists every place it looked, with a button to set the path. A <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.path</code> that does not exist is reported, not replaced by another <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Settings</h3>
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left text-gray-300">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Setting</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Default</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.enabled</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">true</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">Run the language server.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.path</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">""</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">Absolute path to <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code>. Empty: the search above.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.trace</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">"off"</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">messages</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">verbose</code> traces the LSP traffic in the <strong class="text-white">Soli</strong> output.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lint.enable</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">true</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">Run <code class="text-amber-400">soli lint</code> when the language server is not running (see below).</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lint.onSave</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">true</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">Lint again on every save (fallback only).</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lint.executablePath</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">""</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">Deprecated: read only when <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.path</code> is empty.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.*</code> names are the Nova extension&rsquo;s. <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.path</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lint.executablePath</code> are read from your user settings only: a repository&rsquo;s <code class="text-amber-400">.vscode/settings.json</code> cannot make the editor run another binary. The extension stays off in a workspace you have not trusted.</p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">When the server cannot run</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code class="text-amber-400">soli lint &lt;file&gt;</code> on open and save takes over only when the server is not running: <code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.enabled</code> is off, the <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> found was built without the <code class="text-amber-400">lsp</code> cargo feature (the extension recognises its refusal and does not restart it), or the server stopped five times in three minutes. While the server runs the fallback is off, so each finding is reported once &mdash; the server applies the same rules, path-aware ones included.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Commands</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Soli: Restart Language Server</strong> &mdash; picks up a rebuilt <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Soli: Show Output</strong> &mdash; the server&rsquo;s log and trace.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Soli: Lint Current File</strong> &mdash; the fallback lint, on demand.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Grammars</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        The TextMate grammars live in <code class="text-amber-400">editors/grammars/</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">soli.tmLanguage.json</code>, scope <code class="text-amber-400">source.soli</code>; <code class="text-amber-400">soli-template.tmLanguage.json</code>, scope <code class="text-amber-400">text.html.soli</code>). <code class="text-amber-400">scripts/sync-grammars.sh</code> copies them into the VS Code and Nova bundles, and CI fails when a copy is stale (<code class="text-amber-400">--check</code>). A Rust test fails when the grammar&rsquo;s keywords and the lexer&rsquo;s disagree. Any editor that reads TextMate grammars (Sublime Text, TextMate, JetBrains through its TextMate bundle support) can load these files.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Tree-sitter grammar</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <code class="text-amber-400">editors/tree-sitter-soli/</code> is a tree-sitter grammar for <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> files, for the editors that highlight, fold and indent with tree-sitter (Neovim, Helix, Zed, Emacs 29+): highlights, locals, folds, indents and injections queries ship with it. A <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> view uses the <code class="text-amber-400">embedded_template</code> grammar ERB uses, with the queries in <code class="text-amber-400">template-queries/</code>: the markup is HTML, and the code of every tag is Soli, read as one program &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;% if x %&gt; &hellip; &lt;% end %&gt;</code> is one <code class="text-amber-400">if</code>. In this repository 96% of the <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> files and every <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> view parse without an error; CI keeps the <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> share at 95% or more and fails when the committed parser is not the one <code class="text-amber-400">grammar.js</code> generates. The grammar is built from the Soli repository (subdirectory <code class="text-amber-400">editors/tree-sitter-soli</code>); it is not yet in nvim-treesitter, Helix or Zed&rsquo;s own registries. Setup for each editor: <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/soli_lang/tree/main/editors/tree-sitter-soli" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">the grammar&rsquo;s README</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Nova <span class="text-base font-medium text-gray-400">(macOS)</span></h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The Nova extension lives under <code class="text-amber-400">editors/nova/soli.novaextension/</code> and uses Nova's <code class="text-amber-400">LanguageClient</code> API to spawn <code class="text-amber-400">soli lsp</code> on stdio.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Install from source</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Build and install the <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> binary so the LSP backend is available, then symlink the extension into Nova's extensions directory:
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="bash"><code class="language-bash text-sm">cargo install --path . --locked

ln -s "$PWD/editors/nova/soli.novaextension" \
  "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Nova/Extensions/com.solilang.soli.novaextension"</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Restart Nova and open any <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> file — diagnostics, hover, and completion should activate within a second. <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> templates are sent to the server as the <strong class="text-white">Soli Template</strong> language, for their diagnostics.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Install from the Extension Library</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Once published, just open Nova → <strong class="text-white">Extensions</strong> → search <strong class="text-white">Soli</strong> → <strong class="text-white">Install</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Settings</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Configure via <strong class="text-white">Nova → Preferences → Extensions → Soli</strong>:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left text-gray-300">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Setting</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.enabled</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Toggle the language server. Off falls back to grammar-only highlighting.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.path</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">Absolute path to <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code>. Leave blank to use <code class="text-amber-400">PATH</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-400">soli.lsp.trace</code></td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3">LSP trace verbosity (<code class="text-amber-400">off</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">messages</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">verbose</code>).</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 mb-12">
        <p class="text-amber-200/90 text-sm">
            <strong class="text-amber-300">Heads up:</strong> Nova doesn't inherit your shell's full <code class="text-amber-300">PATH</code>. If the server doesn't start, set <strong class="text-white">Soli &gt; Soli binary</strong> to the absolute path of <code class="text-amber-300">soli</code> (for example, <code class="text-amber-300">/Users/you/.cargo/bin/soli</code>) and run <strong class="text-white">Editor → Extensions → Restart Soli Language Server</strong>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Neovim</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Neovim 0.11+ configures the server natively. Two things are needed besides the server itself: <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> is S-Lang (<code class="text-amber-400">slang</code>) to Neovim by default, so map it to a <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> filetype first &mdash; and <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> to a filetype of its own, so templates reach the server for their diagnostics &mdash; and <code class="text-amber-400">nvim-lspconfig</code> has no <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> entry, so declare the server yourself:
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="lua"><code class="language-lua text-sm">-- ~/.config/nvim/after/plugin/soli.lua (or anywhere in your config)
vim.filetype.add({ extension = { sl = "soli", slv = "soli_template" } })
-- Highlight templates as HTML with embedded code.
vim.treesitter.language.register("embedded_template", "soli_template")

vim.lsp.config("soli", {
  cmd = { "soli", "lsp" },
  filetypes = { "soli", "soli_template" },
  root_markers = { "soli.toml", "config/routes.sl", ".git" },
})

vim.lsp.enable("soli")</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        For highlighting with tree-sitter instead of Vim&rsquo;s syntax files, build the <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/soli_lang/tree/main/editors/tree-sitter-soli" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Soli grammar</a> and copy its queries; its README has the lines, and the ones that read <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> tags as Soli rather than ERB&rsquo;s Ruby.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Open a <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> file and <code class="text-amber-400">:checkhealth vim.lsp</code> lists the <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> client. With Neovim&rsquo;s default mappings, <code class="text-amber-400">K</code> hovers, <code class="text-amber-400">CTRL-]</code> jumps to the definition, <code class="text-amber-400">grr</code> lists references, <code class="text-amber-400">grn</code> renames and <code class="text-amber-400">gra</code> offers the code actions; format with <code class="text-amber-400">:lua vim.lsp.buf.format()</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Helix</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">In <code class="text-amber-400">~/.config/helix/languages.toml</code>:</p>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="languages.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[language-server.soli]
command = "soli"
args = ["lsp"]

[[language]]
name = "soli"
scope = "source.soli"
file-types = ["sl"]
roots = ["soli.toml", "config/routes.sl"]
comment-token = "#"
language-servers = ["soli"]

[[language]]
name = "soli-template"
scope = "text.html.soli"
file-types = ["slv"]
roots = ["soli.toml", "config/routes.sl"]
grammar = "embedded-template"
language-servers = ["soli"]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">For highlighting, add the <code class="text-amber-400">[[grammar]]</code> entry and the queries from the <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/soli_lang/tree/main/editors/tree-sitter-soli#helix" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">grammar&rsquo;s README</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Debugging</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12"><code class="text-cyan-400">soli dap</code> is the debug adapter: breakpoints (conditions, hit counts, logpoints), stepping and variables for a script or the specs, and <code class="text-cyan-400">soli serve --dev --inspect</code> to attach to a running server. The VS Code extension declares the <code class="text-cyan-400">soli</code> debug type; Neovim (nvim-dap), Helix and any DAP client work too. Setup and limits: <a href="/docs/development-tools/debugging#step-debugger" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Debugging &rarr; Step debugger</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Other Editors</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Any editor with an LSP client can start <code class="text-amber-400">soli lsp</code> on stdio for <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> files. The shape of the entry, in the style most clients use:
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Generic LSP Config"><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "name": "soli",
  "command": ["soli", "lsp"],
  "filetypes": ["soli", "soli_template"],
  "fileExtensions": [".sl", ".slv"],
  "rootPatterns": ["soli.toml", "config/routes.sl"],
  "languageId": "soli"
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        The server tells a template by its <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> extension, whatever language id the client sends. It reads the application root from the workspace folders the client sends in <code class="text-amber-400">initialize</code>, or from the nearest <code class="text-amber-400">soli.toml</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">config/routes.sl</code> above the open file.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Requirements</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-6 mb-12">
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2">
            <li>The <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> binary on your PATH (<code class="text-amber-400">soli --version</code> should work), or its absolute path in the editor&rsquo;s settings</li>
            <li>Editor files should use the <code class="text-amber-400">.sl</code> extension; <code class="text-amber-400">.slv</code> view templates must be sent to the server too (see the Neovim and Helix setups)</li>
            <li>For project root detection, include a <code class="text-amber-400">soli.toml</code> file in your project</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Next Steps</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <a href="/docs/development-tools/linting" class="group block p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 hover:bg-white/10 hover:border-amber-500/50 transition-all">
